<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Duy Tân</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1900">1900</a>–<a href="/years/1945">1945</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Vĩnh San</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Duy Tân was emperor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vietnam">Vietnam</a> of the Nguyễn dynasty from 1907 to 1916. Placed on the throne as a child after the French deposed his father <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thanh-thai">Thành Thái</a>, he grew opposed to French rule and in 1916 joined a planned uprising by Vietnamese troops. The plot was discovered, and he was deposed and exiled to Réunion; he died in a plane crash in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a> in 1945.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/duy-tan.jpg" alt="Duy Tân" width="320" height="439" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Duytan_on_throne.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Duy Tân was born Nguyễn Phúc Vĩnh San on 19 September 1900, the 11th emperor of the Nguyễn dynasty. His father was the emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thanh-thai">Thành Thái</a>, who opposed French rule. Because of that opposition and his erratic actions, which some speculate were feigned to shield his opposition from the French, Thành Thái was declared insane and exiled to Vũng Tàu in 1907. The French passed the throne to his son, who was only seven years old. They hoped that someone so young would be easily influenced and raised to be pro-French.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>The prince was enthroned with the reign name Duy Tân, meaning friend of reform, but the French efforts to raise him to support them largely failed. As he grew older he noticed that, although he was treated as emperor, it was the colonial authorities who were obeyed. As a teenager he came under the influence of the mandarin Trần Cao Vân, who opposed the colonial administration, and they began to plan a secret rebellion with others.</p><p>In 1916, while <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> was preoccupied with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">World War I</a>, Duy Tân was smuggled out of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/forbidden-city">Forbidden City</a> with Trần Cao Vân to call on the people to rise against the French. The secret was revealed, France sent troops, and after only a few days they were betrayed and captured. Because of his age, Duy Tân was deposed and exiled rather than killed, while Trần Cao Vân and the other revolutionaries were beheaded.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Duy Tân was exiled to Réunion with his father and continued to favour national liberation for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vietnam">Vietnam</a>. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a> he resisted the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vichy">Vichy</a> regime, then joined the Free French Forces, rising from second lieutenant in December 1942 to lieutenant-colonel in September 1945. In late 1945 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-de-gaulle">Charles de Gaulle</a> spoke to him about returning to Vietnam as emperor, but he died in a plane crash in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-africa">Central Africa</a> on the way home. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> posthumously made him a Companion of the Ordre de la Libération. In 1987 his remains were brought back to Vietnam to rest in the tomb of his grandfather, Emperor Dục Đức, and most cities in Vietnam have named major streets after him.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Duy Tân” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Yet the dynasty also produced resistance: the Can Vuong (‘Aid the King’) movement launched in the name of the boy emperor Ham Nghi in 1885, and the emperors Thanh Thai and <b>Duy Tan</b>, deposed and exiled by the French in 1907 and 1916, became patriotic symbols.</blockquote><a href="/empire/nguyen-dynasty">Nguyen dynasty · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>France deposed emperors who resisted, including Ham Nghi, who led the Can Vuong movement from 1885, and later Thanh Thai and <b>Duy Tan</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/french-protectorate-of-annam">French protectorate of Annam</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Imperial City of Hue, in what is now <a href="/countries/vietnam">Vietnam</a>. Died in Lobaye Prefecture, in what is now <a href="/countries/central-african-republic">the Central African Republic</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=16.47,107.58&amp;year=1929" aria-label="Imperial City of Hue on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Duy Tân"><image href="/maps/globes/g120n30.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M3989 6091h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M3989 6091h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=16.47,107.58&amp;year=1929">What was here in 1929? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
